# Build output — never hand- or Prettier-formatted, and it's already excluded from git.
# Listed here explicitly rather than relied on via .gitignore fallback: Prettier's CLI defaults
# its --ignore-path to [.gitignore, .prettierignore], so dist/ is *currently* also skipped
# implicitly, but that's an accident of the default flag value, not a guarantee — an explicit
# entry here means the build output stays excluded even if --ignore-path is ever overridden.
/dist

# npm-managed lockfile. Its formatting is owned by npm's own writer, not by us — it is never
# hand-edited, and running `npm install` can rewrite it in ways that don't match Prettier's
# JSON style, which would make `format:check` fail on a file no one touched. Excluded on
# principle even though it currently happens to already match Prettier's output.
package-lock.json

# node_modules is ignored by Prettier's own built-in default (see --with-node-modules in
# `prettier --help`) — not listed here, no need to duplicate it.

# public/ is deliberately NOT listed: today it holds only a binary favicon Prettier has no
# parser for and therefore already leaves alone, but anything textual added there later
# (a manifest, an SVG) is ordinary source and should be formatted like the rest of the tree.

# --- Genuine Prettier bug, not a style choice — do not remove without re-testing first ---
# All three contain a Vitest `it(name, fn, timeoutMs)` call whose fn and the trailing numeric
# timeout are separated by a multi-line `//` comment. Prettier 3.9.6 is provably non-idempotent
# on that shape: a single `--write` does not reach a fixed point, and each further `--write`
# merges one more comment line onto the same line in *reversed* order, only stabilizing after
# 4 passes with the three original comment lines mashed into one, reordered, line — a real
# content change, not layout. Reproduced directly (diff between successive --write passes was
# non-empty three times running) before excluding rather than papering over it. Left in their
# original hand-formatted state; fixing it for real means moving the comment out of that
# position (e.g. above the `it(` call), which is a hand-edit out of scope for a formatting-only
# sweep. See frontend-solid.md F10.
src/app/features/asset-detail/asset-detail.page.spec.ts
src/app/features/portfolio/components/holdings-table.spec.ts
src/app/features/transactions/transactions.page.spec.ts
